{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for exact head a0f6b0055c533e7a1ab805f4bf6eabbdc0784480.\n\nBlocking defect: TestFirstRunSetupIsLocked does not establish that the forge service carries an effective lock. It searches the whole Compose file with strings.Contains, including comments and other services. I commented out the only GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK mapping, verified the mutation in the diff, and `go test -count=1 ./spike/forge` still passed. The resulting forge boots without the lock, so this directly violates the review condition that the regression catch an unlocked first run. Parse the forge service's environment (or implement an equivalently scoped, comment-aware check) and pin this commented-out mutation.\n\nPinned configuration evidence: the key itself is correct. Gitea's official 1.24 Docker documentation defines GITEA__SECTION_NAME__KEY_NAME mapping; the v1.24.6 image setup runs environment-to-ini on every start; v1.24.6 HasInstallLock reads [security] INSTALL_LOCK; and its runWeb selects installed versus installer routes from that value. Docker's official Compose reference also confirms an omitted host IP binds all interfaces and 127.0.0.1 is the correct local-only short form.\n\nWorkflow: the CLI administrator step is supported by Gitea's command-line contract. The login probe distinguishes the pinned version's normal server from install mode. The subsequent `/install` probe is redundant and misleading: v1.24.6 serves the installer at `/`, while unmatched install routes and normal `/install` both return not-found. Remove it or replace it with a check that observes a real state transition. A live boot was unavailable because this host has no Docker daemon or Compose plugin; pinned source and official docs settle the configuration semantics.\n\nArchitecture: this changes the optional operational spike at layer 7 (external surface/deployment lane) and preserves the documented kernel, Workroom, projection, and interface contracts; architecture.md does not need a contract update.\n\nSecurity: explicit loopback binding and INSTALL_LOCK correctly narrow the network and first-admin authority boundaries. The unresolved false-negative regression means the security condition is not yet durable, so approval is unsafe.\n\nSimplification: keep the small scoped regression, but make it parse the effective forge environment and remove the no-op `/install` probe. The manual port-block parser caught bare and 0.0.0.0 mappings; no broader YAML abstraction is needed than the conditions require.\n\nIndependent gates at the exact head passed: go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./spike/... (three packages), uncached make -B docs, git diff --check, clean worktree. Independent mutations of bare 3300:3000, 0.0.0.0, missing lock, and missing admin command were caught; the commented-lock mutation was not.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3a4a92c03d6f6f5824823c97a2575363c10928d3","head":"a0f6b0055c533e7a1ab805f4bf6eabbdc0784480","verdict":"changes-requested"}}